shipping forecasters in and the seas are  looking choppy a crucial tool to  temperature check the global economy the  industry now looks like it's in the eye  of the storm a key gauge for the sector  sunk to a record low at the end of last  month in fact the cost to ship raw  materials around the world is now more  than 80 percent less than its 31 year  historical daily average analysts point  to two reasons firstly there's a glut of  ships out there they were ordered during  the pre-crisis boom years and many are  still being built the number of capsized  vessels for example the biggest ships  transporting 150,000 tons or more have  doubled since 2008 in fact the world's  Freight fleet is expected to grow more  than four percent this year while demand  for the goods they transport will grow  less than 2 percent and older ships  which would have once been demolished  are being kept in service because the  scrap steel prices are too low all that  extra capacity has crushed freight rates  it's now cheaper in fact to hire an  eleven hundred foot merchant vessel than  rent a Ferrari f40 for the day then  there's China slowing growth meant there  were actually fewer ships importing iron  ore and coal into the country in 2015  compared to the year before the first  time that's happened in at least a  decade looking out onto the horizon many  expect more storms ahead for shipping  and the global economy
 

